# Manuscript PDF page 13

                                    3:07



   Let me set the room, since the room matters. Fourth floor, back of
the building, over Jerome Avenue, so the 4 train comes past the win‐
dow every eight minutes like it’s late for something. My desk was dev
boards, cables, and a Pyrex of arroz con habichuelas I’d been about to
return to the lady on three since February. The chair in the corner had
enough laundry on it to count as a roommate. The window stayed
cracked, winter or not, because I can’t work in quiet. People hear that
and think it’s a flex. It isn’t. Quiet is when my brain finally gets a turn
to talk, and the first thing it does is read me a list of everything I
haven’t finished. So I kept the world on: the train, the bodega radio
down the block, the radiators knocking, my mother’s rosary clicking
two rooms over (in her sleep; I’ve tested this), all of it running like a
fan in a hot room.
   Starting is the other problem. I can’t start anything in a silent room,
and I can’t start anything on purpose. I start things on the train. When
the 4 goes past the window, that’s the gun, and I have eight minutes
until the next one to get a first line on the screen. Every project I’ve ever
shipped was built in eight-minute sprints, a train at a time, and I’ve
never told anyone, because it sounds like a system and I’m embar‐
rassed by anything that works.
   On the nightstand sat the coin.
   Copper-colored, heavy for its size, cut with spirals and little hooked
triangles my grandmother calls señales viejas, old signs, in the voice
that means I’ll tell you when you’re ready and I’m not sure you will be.
She gave it to me when I was twelve, after a fever that had me talking
in my sleep for three days and woke me up with dirt under my finger‐
nails, on the fourth floor of a building that has no dirt in it. Everybody
in my family acted normal about that. In my family, acting normal is
the loudest way to say something matters.
   The coin ran warm. Not body-warm, not radiator-warm. Off, like a
plate somebody held a minute too long before handing it to you. I’d
pressed it to the back of my hand maybe a hundred times trying to
prove I was imagining it. I wasn’t. I had it filed, by then, in a folder in
my head labeled coincidence, along with the other forty-six things I
couldn’t explain. Forty-seven if you count the coin. I counted the coin.



